WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.470
An advert says the average customer

2
00:00:03.470 --> 00:00:06.730
uses two gigabytes of data a month.

3
00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:10.160
Our class checked: most of

4
00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:11.640
us use less than one.

5
00:00:12.370 --> 00:00:14.390
How can both be true?

6
00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:19.080
Today: mean, median and range, and

7
00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:20.540
summaries that tell the truth.

8
00:00:21.690 --> 00:00:24.190
Here are three ways to sum up numbers.

9
00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:27.420
The mean: add them all, and

10
00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:28.680
divide by how many.

11
00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:31.630
The median: sort them, and

12
00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:32.470
take the middle one.

13
00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:36.250
The range: the biggest minus the smallest.

14
00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:40.410
Five friends' data in a month: one,

15
00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:44.490
one, two, three and nine gigabytes.

16
00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:47.170
The mean is three point two.

17
00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:49.210
The median is two.

18
00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:52.170
One heavy user drags the mean

19
00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:54.170
up; the median stays put.

20
00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:57.120
So which should you use?

21
00:00:57.850 --> 00:01:00.110
The mean, when the numbers are similar.

22
00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:03.050
The median, when a few are far

23
00:01:03.050 --> 00:01:04.970
from the rest, like incomes,

24
00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:07.570
house prices or data use.

25
00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:09.290
Quick check!

26
00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:13.350
Marks of eleven, twelve, thirteen,

27
00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:17.190
fourteen, and one typed as one hundred and

28
00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:17.510
fifty.

29
00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:20.500
Which summary is still about right?

30
00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:23.440
The median: thirteen.

31
00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:26.910
The mean jumps to forty, which is

32
00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:28.830
impossible for marks out of twenty.

33
00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:31.490
That's your clue to look for a typo.

34
00:01:32.710 --> 00:01:35.710
In Python, sorted puts a list in order.

35
00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:39.340
The middle item is the median, and

36
00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:41.760
sum divided by len is the mean.

37
00:01:43.010 --> 00:01:45.090
Summaries need a chart beside them.

38
00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:47.180
Bars start at zero.

39
00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:49.810
Give the chart a title, and

40
00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:51.110
units on the axis.

41
00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:53.630
And say where the data came from.

42
00:01:54.850 --> 00:01:56.670
In the app, a week of soil

43
00:01:56.670 --> 00:01:58.510
readings with a few glitches.

44
00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:00.780
Leave them out, and see

45
00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:01.920
which summaries move.

46
00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:05.050
The lowest jumps from zero to about

47
00:02:05.050 --> 00:02:07.990
fifty-five, but the median doesn't move.

48
00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:10.100
Your turn.

49
00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:14.020
Which of these makes a bar chart unfair?

50
00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:17.820
Bars that don't start at zero make

51
00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:19.880
small differences look huge.

52
00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:23.180
Remember: the mean adds and

53
00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:25.740
divides; the median is the middle.

54
00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:29.120
One extreme value drags the

55
00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:30.680
mean, not the median.

56
00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:33.860
And an honest chart starts at zero.

57
00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:37.900
Try it tomorrow: find an average

58
00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:40.100
in an advert or the news.

59
00:02:40.850 --> 00:02:44.230
Ask: is it a mean or a median?

60
00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:47.380
And what could be dragging it?
